  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1141 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nScattered showers and isolated thunderstorms will come to an end \novernight as a cold front moves across the area. Clouds will be\non the decrease overnight into early Thursday morning. High pressure\nwill then build into the region Thursday afternoon. Abundant low \nlevel moisture may result in skies becoming mostly cloudy again\nduring the day. Later in the day, mid-level moisture may result in\npartly to mostly cloudy skies Thursday night. Tranquil conditions\nshould prevail through Saturday morning. There is still a great\ndeal of uncertainty on the northward extent of the rain, 20-40%\nacross central and east-central WI, on Saturday as the \nCanadian/ECMWF solution was drier than the GFS.\n\nDry conditions should prevail Sunday into Monday morning, before\nthe next chance of rain (20-50%) arrives Monday afternoon. A more\npotent system will move into the central plains on Monday, then\nmove into the Great Lakes region Monday night and Tuesday which \nwill bring a soaking rain to the area. Current rain chances are\n50- 70%, but will likely go up in the coming days. High pressure\nthen build into the region next Wednesday. Temperatures will\nremain above normal over the next week. with above normal\ntemperatures are likely for the month of November and the upcoming\nwinter (Dec-Feb period). Also, we turn the clocks back Sunday\nmorning as we switch to Central Standard Time. That means sunsets\nbefore 5 pm!.",
